        "thinking": "`provider-registry.ts` imports `s3-provider.ts` at the top. And this file might be imported by the server routes. Let me check.\n\nActually, let me check where `provider-registry.ts` is imported from, since neither routes nor services seem to import it.\n\nWait, looking at `boot.ts`:\n```ts\nimport { registerStorageProvider } from \"../../../server/src/storage/provider-registry.js\";\n```\n\nSo `boot.ts` imports `provider-registry.ts`, which imports `s3-provider.ts`. And `api.ts` imports `boot.ts` (via `bootCloudflare`). So the S3 provider IS in the dependency chain.\n\n\nThe issue is that `provider-registry.ts` statically imports `s3-provider.ts` at the module level, which pulls in the entire AWS SDK into the bundle even though `boot.ts` only uses the `registerStorageProvider` function. For Cloudflare workers, I need a CF-specific version of `provider-registry.ts` that avoids importing S3 and local-disk providers entirely.\n\nI could either mark AWS SDK packages as external and improve the Node.js stubs, or create a separate registry for CF workers. The second approach is cleaner architecturally, but the first is faster to implement. I'll go with improving the stubs to handle the AWS SDK dependencies more comprehensively.\n\nExpanding the fs stubs...",
